# Claude Code workflow reference

Env-specific facts and decisions only, each at its current state with a dated
annotation. Generic harness behavior is injected into every session or
answerable live via the claude-code-guide agent; measurements, research and
audit baselines live in the chezmoi repo's `docs/agents-audit-log.md`. If
behavior seems changed, verify with that agent and update this file.

## Models (last verified 2026-09-15)

- Global model is pinned in settings (`model` key) from the chezmoi repo as
  `{{ .agents.claude.defaults.model }}`; `/model` changes only the current
  session. The pin is the frontier tier as the session driver (decision
  2026-09-15); `[1m]` holds the 1M-context variant — without it Fable 5 runs
  at 200K. The pin follows the stable channel, which cannot yet serve 5.1
  (floor and probe record on the pin in agents.yaml): bump it and
  `subagent_tiers.claude.frontier` together once stable clears the floor;
  driver effort stays the Claude Code default (lower effort unmeasured on Max).
- Pins use explicit model IDs, never bare aliases: aliases re-resolve across CLI releases.
- Subagent tiers are relative capability, pinned per specialist in
  `.chezmoidata/agents.yaml` (`subagent_tiers.claude`); they do NOT follow
  `/model`. Unpinned built-ins (general-purpose, Plan, claude,
  claude-code-guide) take `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, set in settings `env`
  to the top worker tier (`{{ .subagent_tiers.claude.top }}`); an unavailable
  model silently falls back. Forks (`subagent_type: fork`) always run the
  parent model — a driver-context continuation, not a worker.
- Subagent model resolution, in order (vendor sub-agents doc, re-verified
  2026-09-15): the per-invocation `model` parameter, the definition's `model`
  frontmatter (`inherit` = the main conversation's model), then
  `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, then the main conversation's model. Omitting
  `model:` lets the env var win — the intended path; no roster entry uses
  `inherit`. An undocumented `_FORCE` variant exists in the binary; leave it
  unset — it would erase the specialists' pins.
- No child runs the frontier tier: `~/.claude/hooks/deny-frontier-child.mjs`
  (PreToolUse, matcher `Agent`, rendered from agents.yaml) denies a per-call
  `model` of the frontier pin, `fable` or `inherit`; hooks from settings files
  run inside subagents too (vendor hooks doc, 2026-09-15). Residual the hook
  cannot see: a third-party agent definition declaring `model: inherit` with
  no per-call model — the roster carries none; the transcript sweep's
  children-vs-pins count is the check.
- After each CLI update: dispatch one unpinned child and one with an explicit
  `{{ .subagent_tiers.claude.top }}` override and read `message.model` in the
  transcript, never the child's self-report — the silent upstream failures
  (pins lost on background resume, the env var overriding per-call requests,
  an override served by another model) are not hook-catchable; issue numbers
  in the audit log.
- Every custom subagent loads `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (built-in Explore and Plan
  do not), so the projection groups its driver-only rules under one "session
  driver" bullet. Continue an existing agent with `SendMessage` for follow-ups
  rather than re-spawning: a long-lived subagent keeps its context and pays
  cache-read rates (Fable 5 guide).
- Subscription limits (Max plan; re-checked 2026-09-14 against the Fable
  support article): a rolling 5-hour window plus a shared weekly pool "shared
  across all models"; Fable draws the same pool, capped at up to 50% of weekly
  usage and "using limits faster than other Claude models", exact weighting
  unpublished. The errors doc still names Opus and Sonnet sub-limits — next
  time a limit binds, reconcile against `/usage`.
- Delegation economics (framing 2026-09-15; baselines in the audit log):
  subagents draw the same Max pools, drain is model-weighted, and the driver's
  context re-meters at Fable's weight every turn with cache reads counting —
  delegation pays only via tier arbitrage and a lean driver context; session
  length is the primary cost lever. Exhausting a cheaper model's sub-limit can
  force frontier fallback for routine work (claude-code#55663).
- Roster notes: `implementer` (mid, added 2026-08-28) takes spec-complete
  write work out of the driver's context — dispatch count is an open trigger
  (0 through 2026-09-12); `spec-reviewer` runs at the top worker tier, not
  `inherit` (decision 2026-09-15) — it only proposes, and its weaker
  concurrency recall is covered by `codex-review` on high-stakes surfaces.
- Codex pin caveat: openai/codex#32587 (open 2026-09-15) — a spawned Codex child
  can silently inherit the parent's model/effort; verify via `/status` after each CLI update.
- `codex-review` / `codex-advisor` are read-only Codex consultation for
  high-risk diffs or stuck calls; Claude and Codex meter on independent
  windows, so read-work can offload to Codex while decision ownership stays in
  Claude. The bridge is pinned to `{{ .subagent_tiers.codex.top }}` (`--model`,
  anchored in agents.yaml): review is a worker role, no source shows Astra
  reviewing better, and Astra costs 2.5x and ~3x the time to first token on a
  call that blocks the Claude loop (research 2026-09-15).
- The codex-review trigger is risk-or-breadth, never blanket (decision
  2026-09-03): yield is findings the session actually fixed; the
  survived-findings count accumulates in the audit log — decide the trigger's
  fate once there are ten.

## Codex transport: owned MCP bridge (decision 2026-09-05)

- `codex mcp-server` was deprecated in codex-cli 0.149.1, so the `codex` MCP
  entry spawns `scripts/codex-bridge.mjs` from the chezmoi source repo,
  wrapping the stable `codex exec` / `codex exec review` CLI. Tools: `review`,
  `advise`, `reply`; invocation fixed to read-only sandbox, never approvals,
  high reasoning, top worker tier — callers pick scope only. `review` states
  the scope inside the prompt when custom instructions are given: the CLI
  rejects a PROMPT combined with `--base`/`--uncommitted`.
- MCP transport kept deliberately: the bridge is harness-spawned outside the
  Bash sandbox (the `~/.codex` deny stays intact) and its rendered allow rule
  (`mcp__codex`) makes the tools prompt-free in plan mode (verified
  2026-09-05). Plan mode blocks MCP tools unless allow-listed (`readOnlyHint`
  ignored; upstream #12368 closed "not planned") and Bash has no plan-mode
  exemption, which rules out every Bash-based transport.

## Codex plugin: not installed (decision 2026-09-05)

- OpenAI's official plugin (`openai/codex-plugin-cc`, dissected at v1.0.6)
  stays uninstalled: it spawns the experimental `codex app-server` through
  sandboxed Bash — blocked by the `~/.codex` deny, never prompt-free in plan
  mode, on a protocol documented as changeable without notice. Reconsider only
  if `codex exec` itself breaks AND the sandbox/plan-mode constraints change.

## pi trial vs codex (decision 2026-09-05)

- pi (`earendil-works/pi`, adopted at v0.85.0) runs interactive GPT sessions
  alongside codex until one wins the trial; ChatGPT-subscription OAuth in
  third-party OSS harnesses is OpenAI-sanctioned (last verified 2026-09-05)
  and draws the same plan quota as codex.
- pi ships no permission system: the managed workflow extension leases a
  per-tool srt (`@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime`) worker for every
  `workspace_*` call with a profile rendered from agents.yaml, and its plan
  mode gives read-only planning (verified 2026-09-09).
- Trial decision trigger: revisit at the next `agent-instructions-audit`, or
  sooner once one harness clearly owns all interactive GPT sessions — then
  demote or drop the loser (codex keeps the review-backend role). Not
  decidable as of 2026-09-15 (status history in the audit log).
